re you're such a talent at just  commanding the screen but what i notice  is like you can tell that you're a  really  trained actor that you you're this isn't  your first rodeo so knowing that you've  actually worked in  theater i wonder i was watching i'm like  how is  the amount of dialogue you have to pull  off in the second act and blocking was  that  like one of the most challenging things  you had to do on a set like compared to  like theater work and  you know how much did the theater work  kind of prepare you for a role like this  i mean i i had i had a very short amount  of time over christmas to prepare  i had like 12 12 or 13 days and i was  like  i have to show up on day one  and not be thinking about my lines you  know depending on the role  i'm sort of 50 50 with land learning  like some sometimes there's  there's something to be something  fluid there's something fluid about not  knowing your lines sometimes  like i remember on high fidelity i made  a point of not learning my lines until  the day  because so much of it was about  responding to zoe and just being  emotionally available and they were only  like one lines at a time  but with this was the opposite i was  like i need to really get  off the book so i i had someone come  around to my house  every morning his name is andy buzzio  and he'd look for me at like eight  and we ran the film we've ran the film  hundreds and hundreds and hundreds of  times so when i got the first day  in new orleans and with regina and the  and the guys  and we did off table read i didn't need  a script you know i just was  i could just i just fired the whole  performance off the table because  regina and i spoke about over christmas  needing to be able to jump in and out of  any moment of the film at any time just  because of structurally how  the schedule was going to work so that  was my  you know commitment to malcolm but  commitment to regina in the production  as well just to make sure i was on top  of that  so that's interesting you mentioned  regina so regina king people don't know  amazing actress directing this film it's  her feature directorial debut  can you kind of give me the pros of  working with a director that is an  actress an experienced actress like  herself like maybe some things you  noticed from this  particular experience you would kind of  take with you for your other projects  regina regina was everything about this  project  she she her understanding  her deep connection to the craft of  acting and  process and her sensitivity around  emotional performance  she really guided us man like i i look  back and i'm like  wha uh just like just  that i worked with her you know like it  was a really  really really really special few months  yeah i mean and i i could see like you  guys  give that vibe like everyone there is  giving 150  you know and some films you don't feel  that and this you clearly do  and i think what you did so well with  malcolm x is you see  like different emotions just you present  just through your facial expressions  whether it's paranoia  or just always very serious but also  like is the realist of this group  do you though find because i feel like  maybe the only joy this man maybe ever  feels is like when he's on the phone  with his daughter like do you find much  joy in this man like when you got into  his head like  what is your kind of view on that i  think his cameras in this movie with  that i wanted to make them his  a treasure for him okay in the moments  you know the moments with with with his  girl on the phone  regina and i spoke about being very very  careful about we wanted to find as much  smiling as we could because we knew when  we got into the room  after the roof that the movie was going  to go into a really  heavy feeling and and the direction was  going to  you know the night was just steering off  track so in that beginning being at the  fight and with the prayer and at the  phone box and with the boys in the room  it was really about trying to find as  many moments as we could  grounded in you know the psychology and  and in malcolm's point of view but  just to see him smile because he smiled  so much you know  the archive footage can be really  deceptive if you if you spend as much  time as  i did with malcolm watching him i mean  he beamed  he beamed and he was a funny man like he  was  very very funny good humid sweet and  kind and bashful and  you know a really modest guy and and  there's a misconception you know for for  many people that martin luther king was  the goody and  malcolm was the baddie and and this was  really an opportunity to like  show a side of malcolm to kind of get  rid of that stupid notion  he was so much more than  you know what the the the stereotype can  feel like it is sometimes  yeah and i mean the film totally pulls  that off too  and just wrapping this up with you  kingsley the last thing i want to talk  about  philosophy about this movie is what do  you think the main thing this film is  trying to say what is it trying to speak  to this audience  i don't know but i hope that people get  to the end  and are thinking about some of the  questions that are raised i think if  that  that's happening then we've done our job  you know the the yeah  the conversation between malcolm and sam  about  what it means to be uh a responsible  black celebrity to be to use your voice  to use your platform to  like to just to just to question what  that means  for all of us and for all of us it's  different depending on who you are and i  think if we  somehow managed to  spark or ignite  some conversation around that then i  hope that's what  you know that comes out kingsley that  was uh beautifully said  i'm excited for people if they've  already watched it or haven't  just keep watching this film it's gonna  be talked out so long now it's a  award buzz here uh it's one night miami  on amazon prime now you can watch it  kingsley thank you so much for joinin